s immersed  in the work I had a huge head start in  understanding requirements I had a  strong sense of the priority of features  and I also knew what aspects of their  work were likely to change and stay the  same and this was really helpful because  in all of the chaos and you know time  pressure of this moment it wasn't going  to be possible to have all of the  meetings that it takes to get from zero  to 100 in terms of really understanding  these requirements so after we  determined that there wasn't any  software in the market that was going to  meet the most important requirements  that they had I was able to put together  wireframes for a rails application and  the staff worked with me to fine-tune  them and you know since the MVP we have  built features for programs that they're  not currently running but they're  intending to run really soon and those  have always required a lot more  adjustment after the fact because there  are things that I don't anticipate and  they wind up running the program a  little bit differently than they  imagined in practice so now I try to  hold the line and build features for  work that they are currently doing so  after requirements we needed a timeline  and we had a conversation about this and  it was pretty clear that a solution that  wasn't available in three months wasn't  going to be a viable solution so that  timeline helped us set the scope of work  for the MVP a little bit more  conservatively so you know your small  scrappy nonprofits who are doing the  work on the ground they probably don't  know that software needs to be  maintained I don't think that's like  general knowledge but  as a developer I knew that if I built it  I would be on the hook to maintain it so  that was my biggest consideration in  making the decision to offer to build  the MVP I kind of imagined I would be  you know committing to spend a certain  amount of time every week for bug feet  features for bug fixes and feature  requests probably for about you know  three years would be kind of the minimum  and over those several years I've come  to understand that it's it's not just  those two things there's a lot of  project management a lot of QA some  open-source maintainer stuff as well as  security and uptime issues that are also  priorities and obviously I'm using the  term maintenance really broadly to just  mean everything that has to happen for  software to be able to be used over the  long of over the long haul and we've  also seen this play out with  out-of-the-box software that solutions  that we've implemented for them at NSC  because you know you're not going to be  maintaining the code of that software  but they could need you know  configuration changes down the line in  order to be able to continue to use the  software in the long run so I think for  any sort of project whether you're  building custom software or not it's a  good thing to evaluate the long-term  need and you I I knew lastly that there  would be some amount of training and  support required but I definitely  underestimated it I mean I understand  mated how much time it would take and I  also kind of thought it was going to be  a one-time thing versus an ongoing need  but I did a combination of video demos  and in-person training the video demos  get out of date as you add new features  and with in-person training new staff  come on maybe a staff member hasn't used  a feature in awhile and now they need to  and they don't really remember what we  talked about initially and as you build  features that are more complex it's  usually more efficient to talk about  them in person and I'm consistently  reminded about how important this is  whenever I sit down with the staff  and they told me you know oh it would be  so great if the software could do this  and I'm like oh it can and so I'm just  constantly reminded that if they don't  know how to use features in the software  they're not going to and we've also seen  this play out with other out-of-the-box  software solutions that we've  implemented for them another tech  volunteer Rebecca has become an expert  in the volunteer management CRM that  they use nation builder and the software  would honestly be used in a pretty  limited way  if she hadn't invested all the time and  energy in training them how to use the  software and advising them how to use it  and and getting them set up with things  so I had the motivation I had the plan  and the third thing that was really  critical for the project to be  successful with shared values which  really boils down to you know how do you  work with other people in the community  and how do you view the work that you're  doing and these values appear you know  they were developed for new sanctuary  coalition's proce a legal clinic but I  share them because I think many of them  are transferable to work with other  organizations and they've definitely  provided a really strong foundation for  this project so no ego it's not about us  as volunteers no drama  I mean staff deal with emergencies on a  daily basis and in order to move any  sort of project forward you will have to  do more you know legwork and follow up  an accommodation of changing priorities  and so it's important to just empathize  with the challenges of leadership are  facing no Savior complexes as NSC  volunteers we are standing in solidarity  with people who have more courage than  most of us will ever have you when our  friends argue their asylum case in front  of an immigration judge  when they walk into a nice check-in not  knowing if they will come back out when  they are imprisoned for months or years  fighting their deportation they're  putting their body on the line for their  right and the right of others to live in  dignity so our friends are leading this  movement and we are standing behind them  and in the work that you do with  organizations it's really important to  take leadership from impacted community  members problem-solve  so as technologists we have this broad  perspective on technology which can be  really useful in solving problems we  don't just have the languages and  technology stacks on our resumes we kind  of understand how it all fits together  and we're not afraid of it and it's  really important to recognize the value  in that because the important work isnt  the sexy work and it's not necessarily  the work that uses what you think of as  your most valuable skills supporting a  migration 2g suite isn't sexy but if  staff are losing tons of important  emails from funders and lawyers to spam  it might be the most important work in  the moment helping someone get set up  with a password manager is not going to  use your programming or design skills  but it's so critical to protect the  community so you know in addition to  these two things we've had new sanctuary  coalition tech volunteers reimage  donated laptops maintained redesign and  then ultimately migrated like a static  information site and as well as set up a  volunteer management CRM which meant  that they were able to accept donations  securely that they could schedule and  accept RSVPs to trainings that they  could send bulk emails and segmented e  multiple interior's you know that said  it is important to learn  respect your own boundaries and what you  can commit to because not doing that  hurts you but it also ultimately hurts  the organization and lastly you know  total communication there's a lot to be  said for you know being in person as a  volunteer as the basis of your work as a  technologist because you learn how the  organization works who you need to talk  to what's the best way to communicate  with them and you do that without  putting you know additional burden on  staff to provide you with the answers to  these questions and at the same time you  build mutual trust with the people that  you're working with which you know is  really important for good communication  so we had motivation plan shared values  we were able to build the MVP with  functionality that addressed some of the  problems that we talked about earlier we  obviously wanted a central place for  admins be able to access digital records  for the friends a Tennessee is working  with so here they can you know search by  various fields we wanted them to be able  to edit these records obviously we want  them to be able to add upcoming ice  check-ins or court hearings anything  that the friend would be a company -  that's the activities here we wanted  admins to have a running list of all of  the the drafts of asylum applications  and other applications that have been  worked on in clinic you know attach to  the friends record and we wanted  volunteers to be able to upload these  documents for the friend that they were  working with and to do that they needed  to be able to be given access a limited  limited amount of access to a specific  friends record that they were working  with in clinic and this is from the  volunteers perspective they're working  with this friend and clinic they can  view all of the documents that have been  uploaded for the friend to date and then  they can upload new documents when they  have a new version at the end of clinic  and obviously the accompaniment program  volunteers you  now able to RSVP view all the  accompaniments for the next couple weeks  and RSVP to accompany so you know the  program's continued growing and with  support from this software and new  sanctuary coalition's other tech efforts  so you know starting with accompaniments  generally we were doing accompaniments  for about ten friends each week in  August 2017  now we're organizing accompaniments for  between 50 to 70 friends each week  likewise starting with 30 volunteers  accompanying weekly in August 2017 by  summer 2019 you know were somewhere  between 125 175 volunteer shifts being  filled weekly in New York likewise the  pro se legal clinic in August 2017 we  had around 40 friends attending clinic  each week that spiked 150 in spring  before we restructured clinic to keep  the numbers around 100 and make it more  sustainable in the long long term and  the volunteer numbers aren't up here but  we at this point have around 300  volunteers attending clinic every week  in New York and you know once we started  to use the software in production it  became more and more integral part of  new sanctuary coalitions day to day work  and because of that you know it was  really imperative to make it sustainable  in the long term because they didn't  start from a sustainable place my  company does summer fridays so I'd work  half-day Friday Saturday Sunday and then  two nights during a week for about two  and a half months to get the MVP out and  on Sundays I'd get together with a  couple other radical coders and we just  hack on the project for a couple hours  which was huge morale boost so we got to  August 2017  got the got the software deployed got  the users migrated and all the user  invitations sent and then I was I was  really burnt out for a couple months and  you know I wanted to get other  contributors on the project to make it  more sustainable and so I thought hack  events I can I can rope in some  developers there so in August our sorry  in December we participated in our first  hack event it was hosted by progressive  hack night in New York and to get ready  for this I did you know a lot of the  things that first-time contributors need  like good seed data a good readme easy  setup and it also really influenced the  way that I wrote issues because I made a  couple of assumptions I assume that the  developers would not have any experience  with this software they wouldn't know  the codebase I was assuming that I  wouldn't know the experience level that  they would be bringing in and I also  kind of knew from experience that you  know miscommunication around what what  we're building and how we're building it  it really sucks and can lead to a lot of  unusable work and I wanted to avoid that  so to compensate for all of those things  I just started writing really  prescriptive issues that just included a  lot of very specific implementation  details and they took a long time to  write but I thought you know this is  just it's just an investment to get this  long-term contributors that I wanted but  heading into summer 2018 you know we  needed to make the app multi-tenant we  needed to make some changes to support  clinics outside of New York and I kind  of divided that work up into a couple  phases I did a solo sprint to make the  multi-tenant changes which is that big  spike in the middle and then the project  was featured at Ruby for good and we had  a team of engineers all working on the  project for a couple days and knocked  out some of the functionality for  clinics outside of New York and then I  had a final sprint to finish everything  up and get it all merged and deployed  and you know of course after that I was  just burned out again  and you know I realized that I wasn't I  wasn't meeting my goal of getting these  long-term contributors and I had been  putting all of this effort into making  the project just work really well for  HACC events but I wasn't putting any  effort into retaining contributors after  I was introduced to them at these events  so I started thinking about you know  what motivates someone to contribute  long term like what motivated me and  what motivated my current and former  co-workers Tasha and Russell who had  begun to contribute more regularly over  the past year and you know how could I  build on this and make it part of the  experience for everyone so I mentioned  before that you I was really motivated  by community by the new sanctuary  coalition community and that was true of  many of the other volunteers and in  reflecting on how NSE has been so  effective in building this community you  know I realized that there were a couple  different aspects of working in this  community that I wanted to extend and  replicate and the first of those is  connection to the work  so as an NSC volunteer you are sitting  in a nice facility and waiting with  someone you are sitting across the table  and working together on an asylum  application the person-to-person  connection is like a foundational part  of the community and it occurred to me  that Tasha and Russell had been working  on this project for about a year and  they hadn't seen clinic in person they  hadn't seen any of the programs in  person and so first chance I had I  invited them to come to clinic and to  meet everyone and to see the software  actually being used in person and that's  something that I you know continued with  regular contributors whenever and  however I can get them to New York to  that in-person connection with the work  because building a connection to the  work takes time I mean I had been  volunteering with new sanctuary  coalition for three years before I wrote  any code and I realized that one thing  you know but but during that time I I  kept getting email updates from new  sanctuary coalition I kept coming back  eventually I became part of the  community and I really said one thing I  had done with Tasha and Russell was just  kind of keep them in the loop on you  know what was happening at new sanctuary  coalition which was easy to do because  we're friends and we're talking anyway  we just you know kind of come up and so  I realized it was really important for  all the contributors to hear about  what's happening in new sanctuary  coalition on a regular basis so that you  know they can start to build that  connection to the work over time and  become increasingly invested so I  started a new sanctuary coalition tech  monthly newsletter and the first thing  in this newsletter is an update on what  new sanctuary coalition has been doing  this month highlights so that they  understand the work on a deeper level  and you know in addition to  understanding the work more broadly you  I realized it was really important for  them to understand the work the the  impact of their work specifically so  that has definitely informed the way  that I structure issues in the repo when  a contributor picks up an issue the  first thing that they see is a  background section that lays out like  what is the real world need for this  issue what will be better in the world  if this is completed so working together  you know whether it hack events or at  volunteering at NSC in person you know  is an aspect of community that was just  really energizing and I wanted to create  some structure so that this could happen  for this project on a regular basis and  so the first thing was that you know we  host a virtual co-working night each  month on zoom' for a couple hours  contributors can come and go and work  hack  the project and chat and in the monthly  newsletter you know I also shout out all  of the work that we've completed this  month because it's important you know  it's really energizing to see the work  of the tech community as a whole and  what we're getting done together and I  also the monthly newsletter has given me  a structure to provide like regular  invitations for contributors to  participate in the work of the community  so I'm able to preview the current and  upcoming work at a on a regular interval  and you know the last aspect of  community that I want to make sure was  embedded in the project was shared  values and goals so of course you know  we've incorporated new sanctuary  coalition's mission and their values  into our code of conduct but I think you  know these values play out the most in  interactions and decisions that we've  made for example you know we've decided  to include all of the tech projects and  all tech volunteers at NSC in this  newsletter in this community because  it's not just the the code not just the  open source contributions that are are  making the work of his community  possible so because the important work  isnt the sexy work and we want all the  work to be visible and you know if our  goal is to support annecy tech needs  sometimes that's going to involve  prioritizing across different projects  and it's going to involve you know a lot  of different skills not just Ruby and  rails so I hope that you may go out and  decide to be an organization's tech  friend you know go out and find the  organizations in your community that are  led by people who are directly impacted  by the issues that they are working on  and you know organizations who were  doing the work on the ground with no  resources and be part of the  community first and then problem-solve  those big problems or the small problems  and your lastly investing community  because community in your work both as a  technologist and you know as an  organizer is the only way that any of  this is ever going to be sustainable so  thank you I have some links for you you  can learn more about new sanctuary  coalition you can donate to help them  continue to do this work you can donate  to the bond fund we're currently in New  York but we hope to have accompaniment  programs in other states in the future  and if you do a little googling you know  you may find that there is an  accompaniment program run by a different  organization near you you can also you  know contribute to the open-source  project we have open issues at the  moment and learn how you can get  involved with a new sanctuary coalition  tech community more broadly and also  I'll be around after and I would you  know really love to chat with you about  any of this so thank you  [Applause]
 

